Rebase the touch scroll origin at the Click->Axis transition so a hold cannot charge the fling.

TouchGestureController pins its scroll origin m_offset at the touch-DOWN point for the
entire Click phase (the whole time the finger is held), updating it only inside the Axis
branch. Combined with the scrollCaptureThreshold clause - a hold of >=200ms flips the
gesture to Axis on the next motion event regardless of distance - the first synthesized
Axis (wheel) event carries a delta equal to the ENTIRE displacement since touch-down,
including any finger drift accumulated during the hold, rather than the per-frame flick
delta.

The kinetic estimator then converts that into a velocity spike: computeVelocity() sums
deltas over the last ~200ms and divides by the motion span, and a quick flick fits inside
that window, so the oversized first delta stays in the numerator while the denominator is
only the flick's duration. The longer the finger is held before flicking, the more drift
accumulates into that first delta, so the fling launches faster the longer you hold - a
"load power then release" behaviour reported on device (build 590, once the Began-phase
latching made the fling actually arm; before that dead flings masked it).

Fix: on the first Axis event of a gesture, rebase m_offset to the current touch point, so
the first scroll delta is zero and reflects only post-threshold motion; the flick itself
then defines the velocity. The Began event still latches the node (phase, not delta). Costs
one pixelsPerLineStep of initial scroll slop, as on other platforms. The axis-lock logic is
unaffected (it measures from m_start, the down point, deliberately). WEBKIT_TOUCH_AXIS_REBASE=0
restores the stock behaviour for A/B. Must apply AFTER webkit-touch-gesture-began-phase.patch
(same lines).

